                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i (G e)
          %p
          #ExampleProfiniteGroupsEquivalentToCertainTopologicalGroups.num_remark
            %h6 Example
            %p
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
              be the category
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grp"} Grp
              of groups, and let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒜
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{A}
              be the category
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi TopGrp
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} TopGrp
              of
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+group"} topological groups
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Set
                    %mo →
                    %mi CHaus
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set \rightarrow CHaus
              sending a set to the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/discrete+topological+space"} discrete topological space
              on this set gives rise to a fully faithful functor
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Grp
                      %mo →
                      %mi TopGrp
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Grp \rightarrow TopGrp
              = succeed ")" do
                %span.newWikiWord
                  finite discrete spaces are cocompact in CHaus
                  %a{:href => "/nlab/new/finite+discrete+spaces+are+cocompact+in+CHaus"} ?
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#PropositionCategoriesEquivalentToProC"}
              implies that the category pro-
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi FinGrp
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} FinGrp
              of pro-objects in
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi FinGrp
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} FinGrp
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/profinite+group"} profinite groups
          %p
          #RemarkProfiniteGroupsEquivalentToCertainTopologicalGroups.num_remark
            %h6 Remark
            %p
              Though it is less well-known, one can in Example
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#ExampleProfiniteGroupsEquivalentToCertainTopologicalGroups"}
              evidently replace
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi CHaus
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} CHaus
              with any category
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒜
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{A}
              for which there is a fully faithful functor
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "sans-serif"}
                      %mi Set
                    %mo →
                    %mi 𝒜
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathsf{Set} \rightarrow \mathcal{A}
              which preserves finite products and lands in cocompact objects. See
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/discrete+object"} discrete object
              for one general setting in which finite product preserving functors exist.
          %p
          .num_remark
            %h6 Remark
            %p
              Both Example
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#ExampleProfiniteGroupsEquivalentToCertainTopologicalGroups"}
              and Remark
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#RemarkProfiniteGroupsEquivalentToCertainTopologicalGroups"}
              generalise from
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "sans-serif"}
                      %mi Grp
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathsf{Grp}
              to any
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite+product+theory"} finite product theory
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite+product+sketch"} finite product sketch
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite+limit+theory"} finite limit theory
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite+limit+sketch"} finite limit sketch
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "sans-serif"}
                      %mi Set
                    %mo →
                    %mi 𝒜
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathsf{Set} \rightarrow \mathcal{A}
              moreover preserves finite limits.
          %h2#examples Examples
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/profinite+set"}> profinite set
                ,
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/profinite+space"} profinite space
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/profinite+group"}> profinite group
                ,
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/progroup"} progroup
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pro-%C3%A9tale+morphism+of+schemes"}> pro-étale morphism of schemes
                ,
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pro-%C3%A9tale+site"} pro-étale site
            %li
              %p
                Since every (
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dg-coalgebra"}> dg-
                )
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coalgebra"} coalgebra
                is the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/filtered+colimit"} filtered colimit
                of its finite-dimensional subalgebras (see at
                = succeed ")," do
                  %em
                    %a{:href => "coalgebra#AsFilteredColimits"} coalgebra – as filtered colimit
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+dual"} linear dual
                of a (dg-)coalgebra is canonically a pro-object in finite dimensional (
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dg-algebra"}> dg-
                )
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/associative+algebra"}> algebras
                \. This plays a role for instance for constructing
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+structures+for+L-infinity+algebras"} model structures for L-infinity algebras
                = succeed "." do
                  %a{:href => "model+structure+for+L-infinity+algebras#OnProAlg"} there
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pro-category+of+towers"} pro-category of towers
          %h2#applications Applications
          %h3#etale_homotopy_theory Etale homotopy theory.
          %p
            Procategories were used by Artin and Mazur in their work on
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%C3%A9tale+homotopy"} étale homotopy
            theory. They associated to a scheme a “pro-homotopy type”. (This is discussed briefly at
            = succeed ".)" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%C3%A9tale+homotopy"} étale homotopy
            %em homotopy category
            of simplicial sets, i.e., in the pro-homotopy category. Friedlander rigidified their construction to get an object in the pro-category of simplicial sets, and this opened the door to use of ‘homotopy pro-categories’.
          %h3#shape_theory Shape theory.
          %p
            The form of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/shape+theory"} shape theory
            developed by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Sibe+Marde%C5%A1i%C4%87"} Sibe Mardešić
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jack+Segal"} Jack Segal
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pro-homotopy+theory"} pro-homotopy category
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Tim+Porter"} Tim Porter
            and then in further work by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Sibe+Marde%C5%A1i%C4%87"} Sibe Mardešić
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jack+Segal"} Jack Segal
